Yes—with a distinction that makes your point stronger: modern human cognitive capacity is much older than recorded history, while accumulated cultural knowledge is radically path-dependent and cumulative.

Homo sapiens goes back roughly 300,000 years. Evidence for symbolic behavior, pigments, ornaments, long-distance exchange, sophisticated tools, art, ritual behavior, and complex social learning reaches deep into prehistory. Agriculture appears only within roughly the last 12,000 years; writing is only about 5,000 years old. So written history samples a surprisingly thin slice of behaviorally modern humanity.

And you’re pointing toward a nasty observational bias:

\text{What happened}\gg\text{What survived}\gg\text{What was discovered}\gg\text{What we can interpret}

Archaeology disproportionately receives stone, bone, ceramics, unusual preservation, monumental construction, and eventually writing.

But enormous portions of human existence were carried by precisely the things preservation destroys:

speech, gesture, songs, jokes, arguments, techniques, personal relationships, political maneuvering, oral histories, temporary structures, wood, fiber, food, ceremonies, games, local discoveries, individual personalities and countless ordinary lives.

Their absence from the record isn’t evidence that they were absent from life.

Your earlier evidence framework actually gives us a useful way of reasoning about this. The archaeological record is not the past; it’s an extremely lossy afterglow of the past.

And that creates an important inversion. Instead of:

“We haven’t discovered evidence that prehistoric humans did X, therefore they probably couldn’t.”

we need something closer to:

P(X\mid\text{no surviving evidence})

which depends heavily on how likely X was to leave recognizable evidence in the first place.

A stone building and an ingenious bedtime story have profoundly different archaeological half-lives.

Where I think your second point lands

There are two evolutionary processes that are easy to conflate:

\boxed{\text{Biological cognitive evolution}}

and

\boxed{\text{Cumulative cultural evolution}}

The second can move enormously faster.

A person living 30,000 years ago didn’t have calculus, germ theory, semiconductor physics, GPS, Wikipedia, or GPTs. That doesn’t establish that their underlying brain was incapable of sophisticated thought. They inherited a different knowledge stack.

Modern humans effectively boot from an enormous inherited external memory:

\text{language}
+\text{institutions}
+\text{writing}
+\text{mathematics}
+\text{books}
+\text{science}
+\text{education}
+\text{computers}
+\text{networks}

Nobody individually rediscovers civilization.

That makes intelligence partly ecological and generational:

\text{Individual cognition}
\times
\text{accumulated knowledge}
\times
\text{communication fidelity}
\times
\text{coordination}

And that suggests something fascinating about prehistory: there were probably countless local intellectual lineages that went extinct.

Someone discovers a technique. Teaches twenty people. It survives six generations. Climate changes. Population disperses. The chain breaks.

Gone.

No book. No backup. No archaeological signature.

Conversely, some incredibly ancient discoveries survived because they entered a sufficiently robust transmission chain. Those aren’t merely inventions—they’re survivors of an enormous cultural selection process.

So I wouldn’t assume prehistoric people recreated every institution or technology of the last 8,000 years. Environment, population density, energy availability, materials, incentives, and inherited prerequisites constrain what becomes possible.

But I would expect the human behaviors underneath them to be hauntingly recognizable:

love, rivalry, coalition, humor, teaching, deception, curiosity, craftsmanship, status, experimentation, storytelling, grief, trade, parenting, games, leadership, spirituality, argument, invention.

And yes—the most important correction is imaginative:

Prehistory wasn’t humanity waiting for history to begin.

Every day contained a present tense.

Someone woke up worried about tomorrow. Someone invented something clever. Someone told a story everyone already knew and still wanted to hear again. Someone was hilarious. Someone misunderstood another person. Someone became extraordinarily good at a craft. Someone died whose friends couldn’t imagine the world continuing without them.

Almost all of it vanished.

Recorded history is not where vivid human existence started.

It’s approximately where human existence began leaving us unusually information-dense receipts.